    "# Lung Nodule Malignancy Classification — 3D CNN (Tuned v1)\n",
    "### Annotated Version — Plain English Explanations\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## What This Notebook Does\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook trains a **3D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)** to classify lung nodules as either **benign** (non-cancerous) or **malignant** (cancerous) using CT scan images.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What is a Neural Network?\n",
    "A neural network is a computer program loosely inspired by the human brain. It learns by looking at thousands of examples and gradually adjusting itself until it can recognize patterns on its own. In this case, it learns what features in a CT scan are associated with malignant nodules.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What is a CNN?\n",
    "A Convolutional Neural Network is a type of neural network specifically designed for images. It uses mathematical filters that slide across the image looking for features like edges, textures, and shapes — similar to how a radiologist scans an image looking for specific visual patterns.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What is 3D?\n",
    "A standard CNN looks at 2D images (flat pictures). A 3D CNN looks at the entire CT volume — a stack of slices — all at once. This lets the model see the full 3D shape and texture of a nodule, which provides more information for classification.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Data\n",
    "- **Source:** LIDC-IDRI dataset — a public collection of lung CT scans with expert radiologist annotations\n",
    "- **Input:** `.nii.gz` files (compressed medical imaging format), one per nodule\n",
    "- **Labels:** `malignant` or `benign` folders (sorted previously by a separate script)\n",
    "- **Total nodules:** ~1,075\n"
